1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,530
How much empathy do you feel towards other people?
你对他人有多少同情心哪？

2
00:00:03,530 --> 00:00:10,330
If you saw someone fall off their bike in traffic, would you stop and help - or just walk away?
如果你看到有人在车流中从自行车上摔下来，你会停下来帮忙，还是直接走开？

3
00:00:10,330 --> 00:00:16,450
Many people would give in to apathy, go about their business and just do nothing.
许多人会选择冷漠，忙于自己的事，什么也不做。

4
00:00:16,450 --> 00:00:27,160
So it was a big surprise when about 100 bystanders got together recently to move a bus in east London to help a cyclist trapped under it.
因此，最近当大约100名旁观者聚集在伦敦东部，一起移动一辆公交车以帮助被困在车下的骑自行车的人的时候，这让人大吃一惊。

5
00:00:27,160 --> 00:00:31,380
A video of the incident went viral on social media.
这一事件的视频在社交媒体上疯传。

6
00:00:31,380 --> 00:00:39,970
According to Zoheb, a driver who stopped his car to take part in the rescue, about five people gathered to move the bus.
据参与救援的司机佐赫布（Zoheb）说，最初大约有五个人聚集在一起来移动公交车。

7
00:00:39,970 --> 00:00:47,590
He says: "There was no chance we could do it, it was more an invitation for other people to help, really".
他说：“我们不可能做到，这更像是邀请其他人来提供帮助。”

8
00:00:47,590 --> 00:00:50,080
The initiative paid off.
这一倡议取得了成功。

9
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:53,140
Diners from nearby restaurants joined in.
附近餐馆的食客加入了进来。

10
00:00:53,140 --> 00:01:01,210
There wasn't much coordination but it didn't take long to develop a collective understanding of what the objective was.
虽然没有太多协调，但人们很快就全部理解了什么是行动目标。

11
00:01:01,210 --> 00:01:05,900
Spontaneous collaboration among strangers doesn't happen often.
陌生人之间的自发合作并不常见。

12
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:10,780
People in a crowd are not sure what to do - they don't have a plan.
群体中的人们不确定该做什么——他们没有计划。

13
00:01:10,780 --> 00:01:21,650
It's one of the reasons bystanders often do nothing, according to... Dr Mark Levine, professor of social psychology at Exeter University in Britain.
英国埃克塞特大学社会心理学教授马克·莱文（Mark Levine）博士表示，这是旁观者经常什么都不做的原因之一。

14
00:01:21,650 --> 00:01:26,250
"The presence of others can inhibit you from helping", he says.
他说：“其他人的存在可能会阻碍你提供帮助。”

15
00:01:26,250 --> 00:01:34,790
The key to positive group behaviour and intervention, Levine explains, "is building a sense of shared identity".
莱文解释说，积极的群体行为和干预的关键是“建立一种共同的身份认同感”。

16
00:01:34,790 --> 00:01:38,730
Action has to be decided quickly, Levine says.
他同时还说，必须迅速决定行动。

17
00:01:38,730 --> 00:01:43,140
"The longer you leave it, the harder it is to make a decision.
“拖得越久，做决定就越难。

18
00:01:43,140 --> 00:01:51,380
If you don't immediately act then you kind of think 'Well, actually I probably couldn't have done anything anyway'."
如果你不立即采取行动，你可能就会想，‘嗯，其实我可能什么都做不了。’”

19
00:01:51,380 --> 00:01:56,370
But the people who took the initiative like Zoheb might make a difference.
但像佐赫布这样主动采取行动的人可能会带来改变。

20
00:01:56,370 --> 00:02:04,560
The cyclist ended up in hospital and the images of the collective effort might inspire others to more acts of solidarity.
骑自行车的人最终被送往医院，而集体努力的画面可能会激励其他人采取更多齐心协力的行动。
